Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have shown great potential in designing theranostic probes for cancer diagnosis and therapy due to their unique properties including versatile structures and composition, tunable particle and pore size, enormous porosity, high surface area, and intrinsic biodegradability. In this work, we show a novel MOF-based theranostic $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 core-shell composites constructed by in situ growth of UiO-66 MOF shell on $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ core for simultaneous drug delivery and magnetic resonance (MR) imaging. In the composites, the UiO-66 shell devotes to encapsulating drug while the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ core serves as MR contrast agent. The $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 core-shell composites show good biocompatibility, high drug loading capacity, progressive drug release, outstanding MR imaging capability as well as effective chemotherapeutic efficacy, demonstrating the feasibility of designing theranostic $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 core-shell composites for cancer diagnosis and therapy.
## Introduction
Metal-organic framework (MOFs) are an emerging class of organic-inorganic hybrid porous materials built from metal ion or cluster nodes and organic linkers which have already been explored for a variety of applications, including separation, catalysis, and gas storage.1,2 In recent years, MOFs have been scaled down to nanometer sizes to form nanoscale MOFs and focused on their preliminary biomedical applications in drug delivery and bioimaging. 3-6
MOFs have been successfully employed as drug delivery vehicles owing to their unique properties suitable for drug loading and release.7 Different from most of the existing pure organic and inorganic carrier materials, MOFs have exceptional high surface area and enormous porosity favorable for entrapment of large amounts of drugs, tunable pore size and hydrophilic-hydrophobic cavities adaptable to host variety of drugs with different physico-chemical properties, controlled host-guest interactions and intrinsic biodegradability. Since Ferey’s group8 first reported the ability of iron-based MOFs as drug carrier to encapsulate ibuprofen molecules at unprecedented levels and deliver drug continuously with no burst effect, many researchers have developed other MOFs such as UMCM-1, ZIF-8, MIL-53, MOF-74, Gd-MOF, UiO-66, CuBTC, and chiral MOFs for drug delivery.9-18
Paramagnetic metal ions-containing MOFs are also promising as contrast agents for magnetic resonance (MR) imagin g.19,20 Compared with clinical small molecule contrast agents, the framework construction features ensure that MOFs not only possess large amounts of paramagnetic metal centers but also exhibit enhanced per-metal-based relaxivity. Lin and co-workers first demonstrated the potential of Gdbased MOFs as MR contrast agents.21 The Gd-based MOFs show excellent longitudinal relaxivity, however, the free Gd3+ ions leaching causes nephrogenic systemic fibrosis,22 which precludes their clinical applications. Given $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions are also known as potent paramagnetic metal ions with much less toxic than $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ ions, low toxic Mn-based MOFs and nontoxic iron-carboxylate MOFs have been developed for $\mathsf{T}_{1}/\mathsf{T}_{2}\cdot$ - weighted MR contrast enhancement.23-25 The biocompatibility of Mn/Fe-MOFs-based MR contrast agents has been improved, but the moderate relaxivity of Fe/Mn-MOFs still limits the imaging sensitivity, which hinders their practical applications. To circumvent the problem and make full use of the extraordinary drug encapsulation capacity of MOFs, the incorporation of nanoparticles with unique magnetic properties into MOFs is indeed an effective strategy.
Iron oxide nanoparticles are prevalent in MR imaging due to their ability to significantly shorten transverse relaxation time and excellent biocompatibility.26 Moreover, various formulations of iron oxide nanoparticles such as ferumoxsil, ferrixan and ferumoxide have already been approved by FDA as the ${\mathsf{T}}_{2}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{M}}{\mathsf{R}}$ contrast agents for clinical use. Recently, $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}.$ - based core-shell nanoparticles such as $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{A u}_{\cdot}$ , $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C u l n S}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{P}\mathsf{D}\mathsf{A}.$ , $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{P P y},$ $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C u}_{2-\times}\mathsf{S},$ , have also drawn considerable attention owing to their superior contrast effect in MR imaging.27-33 For these reasons, the employment of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ as magnetic nanoparticle to design multifunctional MOF-based composites with high relaxivity, large drug payload and good biocompatibility is practically feasible. Recently, two groups have respectively developed multifunctional $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{P A A}/\mathsf{A u N C s}/\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}\cdot\mathsf{8}\quad\mathsf{N}|$ ${\mathsf{N P S}}^{34}$ and RITC$\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{l R M O F}-3/\mathsf{F A}\mathsf{N P s}^{35}$ by coating $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ with two different Zn-based MOFs for imaging and drug delivery, which demonstrate the practicability of MOF-based composite in biomedicine. However, the poor moisture stability of IRMOF-3 and the toxicity of Zn-based MOF originating from the ion channel/DNA damage caused by the competition of $Z n^{2+}$ with $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ hinder their practical application in biomedicine36,37. Furthermore, the synthesis procedures usually include multiple steps, and the capability of MOFbased composites in imaging and drug delivery as well as the toxicity should be further systematically investigated. Thus, further development of novel MOF-based theranostic agent via a simple method is necessary and of great interests.
Herein, we report our initial effort to simply incorporate $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticle with MOF UiO-66 to fabricate novel theranostic MOF core-shell composites $(\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{U i O-}66)$ for in vitro and in vivo MR imaging and drug delivery (Scheme 1). UiO-66 as one class of zirconium-based MOFs is constructed with $Z r(|\vee)$ and 2-amino-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate $(N H_{2^{-}}$ ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}})$ ligands, and has received great attention in drug delivery due to its excellent chemical and solvent stability.12,15 The coordination of $Z r(|\vee)$ -cluster and linear ligands forms a cubic rigid 3D porous structure consisting of octahedral cavities with a diameter of $1.1\ \mathsf{n m}$ and tetrahedral cavities with a diameter of $0.6\ \mathsf{n m}$ .38 The presence of $Z r{-}O$ clusters, numerous open cavities, metal sites and amphiphilic character make UiO-66 advantageous to capture and release anticancer drugs such as doxorubicin (DOX) based on the strong coordination interactions between the hydroxyl groups in DOX and $Z r(|\vee)$ centers in UiO-66. In addition, the toxicity of UiO-66 has been demonstrated to be relatively low.37 Thus, UiO-66, which not only exhibits exceptional chemical and solvent stability but also possesses good biocompatibility, was selected to fabricate the shell on the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles for DOX delivery. $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 core-shell composites were synthesized via a facile in situ growth method based on the controllable growth of a UiO-66 shell on carboxylateterminated $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ core. The formed $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites simultaneously possessed the $T_{2}-M R$ contrast property of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ cores and the drug delivery ability originating from the MOF shells. As a result, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites show excellent stability, high drug loading capacity, low cytotoxicity, negligible in vivo toxicity and obvious MR signal attenuation effect. Furthermore, the DOX encapsulated composites show a sustainable drug release, and exhibit a long-term lasting and efficient anticancer therapeutic efficacy. All the results demonstrate that the developed $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ core-shell composites possess great potential as a novel theranostic agent for MR imaging and drug delivery in biomedicine.
## Results and Discussion
Synthesis and characterization of $\bar{\mathsf{F e}}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and ${\pmb{\mathsf{F e}}}_{3}{\pmb{0}}_{4}@$ UiO-66
$\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were synthesized by a hydrothermal method according to the literature.39 The as-synthesized $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were spherical with a diameter of $150\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. 1A) and in the cubic phase (JCPDS: 19-0629) (Fig. 1E). The $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites were then synthesized by an in situ self-assembly of UiO-66 on the surface of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ to obtain the core-shell theranostic agent. In a typical process, $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were directly dispersed into the synthetic precursor of UiO-66 composed of $\mathsf{Z r C l}_{4},$ $N H_{2}-H_{2}B D C$ and DMF, and the reaction was accomplished through a simple hydrothermal procedure. The developed method avoids timeconsuming layer-by-layer MOF growth and further modification of core particle, which is much more simple than most of the previous synthetic methods of MOF-based coreshell composites.40,41
To control the morphology of the core-shell composites, the concentration of the precursors of UiO-66 was optimized. After mixing with different concentrations of UiO-66 precursor solutions, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites with different UiO-66 shell thickness (5, 25, $50~\mathsf{n m}$ ) were obtained (Fig. S1, ESI†). The $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites show uniform core-shell morphology with a $25{\mathsf{n m}}$ thickness of UiO-66 shell when the ratio of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and UiO-66 precursor reached $25~{\mathsf{m g}}:37.5~{\mathsf{m g}}$ $Z r C l_{4}$ (29 mg $N H_{2}-H_{2}B D C$ / $18~\mathsf{m L}$ DMF) (Fig. 1 B). Either the UiO-66 shell was too thick or no shell was formed if the ratio was higher or lower than the optimized ratio. Thus, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ core-shell composites synthesized under the optimized condition were used for the following experiments. Furthermore, high angle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscope (HAADF-STEM) and elemental mapping analysis were used to verify the core-shell structure of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composite (Fig. 1C and D). A clear contrast between the core and shell was obtained, where the core appears dark while the shell shows bright. Moreover, the EDS (energy dispersive spectrometer) line scanning data and elemental mapping analysis reveal that the Fe and $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ elements were distributed in the core and shell, respectively. All the results demonstrated the successful formation of coreshell structure in $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composite.
The simultaneous existence of the characteristic peaks of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and UiO-66 in the $\mathsf{x}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ indicates the successful formation of UiO-66 shell on the surface of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles without altering their crystallinity (Fig. 1E). The characteristic peaks of UiO-66 at $1570\ c m^{-1}$ , $1435~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , $1386~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ at $1651~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , 595 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ in the flourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectrum of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ indicating the formation of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites (Fig. S2, ESI†). The two peaks at $3416~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $3373~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ in the spectrum of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ , belonged to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching absorptions of the primary amine groups in the $N H_{2}-H_{2}B D C$ ligands, which further verified the successful growth of UiO-66 shell on the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ core. The contents of Fe and Zr elements in the prepared $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ were $30.6\%$ and $0.9\%$ , respectively. Thermogravimetric analysis results showed that the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites decomposed at $340^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , which had a similar breakdown temperature to the synthesized UiO-66 nanocrystals (Fig. S3, ESI†). The hydrodynamic size and surface zeta potential of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites in phosphate buffer solution (PBS) were $241.5\pm28.5\mathsf{n m}$ (polydispersity index ${\bf\varepsilon}=0.340^{\cdot}$ ) and $-25.7\pm5.2\mathsf{m V}$ , respectively (Fig. S4, ESI†). The results indicate the composites had good dispersity and colloidal stability, which ensured their practical application in drug delivery and MR imaging.
The drug loading capacity of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites depends on the porosity feature of the UiO-66 shells. $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were used to characterize the surface area and porosity of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ (Fig. 2A). The total pore volume $(V_{\mathrm{total}})$ and BET surface area $(S_{\mathsf{B E T}})$ of the synthesized $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 were calculated to be $0.21~{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}~{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}$ and 149.75 $\mathfrak{m}^{2}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ , which are large for drug loading despite much lower than the pure UiO-66 due to the interior nonporous $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ core. In addition, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites possessed an interparticle mesopore at $3.5\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Table S1, $\mathsf{E S l f}_{\mathsf{\Pi}}^{\mathsf{\prime}}$ ), which makes them efficient for drug delivery.
Superparamagnetism is essential to MR imaging. Thus, the magnetic property of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites was investigated in a magnetic field range from $-70$ to $+70{\mathrm{~kOe}}$ at room temperature (Fig. 2B). The saturation magnetization (Ms) of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 was 51.58 emu $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ , smaller than that of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles (69.69 emu $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) due to the encapsulation with the UiO-66 layer. The high Ms value and the existed hysteresis loop without significant coercivity and remanence in the magnetization hysteresis curves demonstrated the strong superparamagnetic character of the as-synthesized $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites.42 Furthermore, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ solution could be quickly collected using the magnet (Fig. S5, ESI†).
The strong superparamagnetism of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites ensured their ability as a $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ contrast agent for MR imaging. $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighetd MR images of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ showed an obvious concentration-dependent darkening effect with a high transverse relaxivity $(r_{2})$ of $255.87\mathsf{m M}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ (Fig. 1F), and the $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}_{2}$ values decreased as the thickness of the UiO-66 shell increased due to the reduced ratio of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ core in the composite (Table S2 and Fig. S6, $\mathsf{E S l^{\dagger}}$ ). Furthermore, the $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}_{2}$ value of the prepared $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ $\{1396~\mathrm{mg}^{-1}~\mathrm{mL}~s^{-1};$ ) was much higher than that of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{P A A}/\mathsf{A u N C s}/\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\mathsf{-}8\mathsf{N P s}$ $(53.79~\mathrm{\mg}^{-1}~\mathrm{\mL}~s^{-1}),^{3.}$ 4 and several clinical Fe-based $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighted contrast agents such as ferumoxsil $(72~\mathsf{m M}^{-1}~\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ , ferumoxide $(98.3~\mathsf{m M}^{-1}~\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ and resovist $(150~\mathrm{\mM^{-1}}~\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ .43 These results show the great potential of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ in $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -MR imaging.
## Drug loading/release of ${\sf F e}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites
The stability of the nanocarrier in a wide pH range from basic to acidic is essential for drug loading/release. Thus, we investigated the stability of the as-synthesized $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites at different pH values (4.0, 5.0, 6.0, 7.4, 8.0). No significant changes in the XRD pattern (Fig. 3A) and the morphology (Fig. S7, ESI†) of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites at these pH values were observed. The results indicated that the composites had super stability.
To evaluate the drug loading capacity of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites, a commonly used anticancer drug DOX was mixed with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ in PBS at $\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 8.0$ for $24\ h$ . After removing the excess unloaded DOX, the drug loaded composites $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX were obtained. UV-vis-NIR spectra of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ before and after encapsulation with DOX were measured. The appearance of the characteristic peak of DOX at $500\mathsf{n m}$ in the UV-vis-NIR spectra of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX and the increase of the characteristic peak with the added DOX confirmed the successful loading of DOX in the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites (Fig. 3B). Moreover, the presence of characteristic sorption bonds (labelled with green dash rectangle) of DOX in the spectra of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX also supported the incorporation of DOX molecules into $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 (Fig. 3C).
The drug loading capacity increased from 2.5 to $66.3~\mathsf{w t\%}$ as the amount of DOX increased from 0.15 to $15~\mathsf{m g}$ (Fig. 3D). Furthermore, the drug loading capacity increased with the thickness of the UiO-66 shell (Table S2, ESI†). The DOX loading capacity of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 could reach 66.3 wt% with an ultrahigh loading content of $2.0\ \mathsf{m g}$ DOX per mg composites, which is almost the highest DOX payload among the MOFs carriers.25, 44-46 The impressive result was meaningful in clinical applications because the administration of high dosages could be realized using small amount of composites.
The high drug loading capacity of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites was probably attributed to the large surface area of UiO-66 shell and the interactions between DOX and UiO-66. A remarkable fluorescence quenching of DOX along with color change from brown to wine for the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX dispersion confirmed the strong interaction between DOX and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ (Fig. S5 and S8, $\mathsf{E S l^{\dagger}l}$ ). Potential interactions include $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking effect between the aromatic anthracycline of DOX and the aromatic pore walls of UiO-66, hydrogen bonding between the oxygen atoms of DOX and the amino groups in UiO-66, Van Der Waals forces, electrostatic interactions, and coordination bonding.47 Among the above interactions, stable coordination bonding between the deprotonated hydroxyls in DOX and the numerous Zr sites in UiO-66 framework played the leading role in drug loading, as confirmed by Ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) spectroscopy and Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Fig. 3E and Fig. S9, ESI†). Red shift of the UV-vis spectrum of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-}\mathsf{D O}\mathsf{X}$ in comparison with DOX after DOX loading into $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 indicates the formation of DOX-Fe complex in the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO66 framework, which is also supported by the similar spectral modification of DOX with free $Z r(|\vee)$ ions (Fig. S9). XPS results showed that the binding energy of $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ 3d shifted from 182.76 and $185.12\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ to 182.40 and $184.76\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{\mathrm{-}D O X},$ respectively (Fig. 3E). The binding energy of $Z r3{\mathsf{d}}$ shifted to lower levels after DOX encapsulation could be attributed to the electron transfer due to the binding of DOX to the active Zr sites.48-50. The formation of the coordination bonds between DOX and metal centres (Zr, Fe, Zn) has been also described previously.44,51,52 Owing to the strong coordination bonding of DOX-Zr, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 showed a remarkable and efficient DOX payload.
The DOX release behavior of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX composites were investigated at different pH values (4.0, 5.0, 6.0, 7.4). The time-dependent accumulated drug release (ADR) curves showed a slow and sustained release pattern without any burst effect (Fig. 3F). The release rate would generate a stable drug concentration and provide sufficient time for the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66\mathsf{{\mathrm{-}}D O}\rangle$ X to accumulate in tumor site. As shown in Fig. 3F, about $36.1\%$ and $21.6\%$ of DOX were released in 41 days at $\mathsf{p H}4.0$ and 5.0, respectively, whereas $17.1\%$ and $13.8\%$ of DOX were released at $\mathsf{p H}~6.0$ and 7.4, indicating the sensitivity of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX to acidic tumor microenvironment. The pH-responsive DOX release was controlled by the drug-matrix interactions under acidic condition. At acidic pH, the amino group in DOX was easily to be protonated to offer DOX a positive charge while the surface zeta potential of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ become less negative $(-9.8\pm$ $1.1\mathrm{\mV}$ at $\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 4.0$ , and $-25.7\pm5.2\mathrm{\mV}$ at pH 7.4). Thus, the electrostatic interactions between $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 and DOX were weakened, which promoted the drug release in acidic condition.53 Moreover, the breakage of the coordination bonds between the protonated hydroxyls in DOX and Zr sites under acidic condition would accelerate the DOX release. In addition to the pH dependent DOX release, the endogenous phosphate salts in the endosomes tend to coordinate to the Zr sites, also facilitating the release of DOX from the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites.52,54 These results indicate that $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 nanocarrier could deliver drug to tumor tissue sustainably and effectively, which would be beneficial to diminish toxic side effect and decrease patient discomfort.
## In vitro cytotoxicity
In vitro cell viabilities of different concentrations of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ , $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{\mathrm{-}D O X}$ and free DOX on HeLa cells were evaluated by MTT assay to study the bio-toxicity of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ and the therapeutic effect of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O-}$ 66-DOX. The HeLa cells treated with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 showed no obvious toxicity (nearly $100\%$ cell viability) even at a concentration up to $500\quad{\mathrm{mg/L}}_{\cdot}$ , indicating the good biocompatibility of the synthesized $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites (Fig. 4A). In contrast, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX exhibited significant increase of anticancer activity against HeLa cells with the increase of the loaded DOX concentration (Fig. 4B). Nearly $60\%$ of HeLa cells were killed after incubation with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX even at a low DOX loading concentration of $20~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ and the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX had similar cell toxicity compared with free DOX. Additionally, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX showed greater lethality against HeLa cells after incubating for a longer time $(48\ \mathsf{h})$ , indicating the long-term and sustained DOX release from the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites.
To demonstrate the low toxic and side effects of DOX loaded $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ on normal cells, we further evaluated the biocompatibility of different concentrations of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-D}0$ OX on 3T3 cells by MTT assay (Fig. 4C). As expected, the 3T3 cells treated with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ showed high viability, further indicating the good biocompatibility of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites. Furthermore, the DOX loaded $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 also showed negligible toxicity on 3T3 cells, demonstrating the low side effect of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX on normal cells. Although $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-D O}$ X could be phagocytized by both normal cells and cancer cells, the tremendous difference in cell viability between the HeLa cells and the 3T3 cells may be attributed to the different DOX release rate determined by the cellular microenvironment. The fast reproduction of cancer cell makes the cellular microenvironment acidic, which facilitates DOX release from $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX and thus leads to a high toxicity on cancer cell. These results demonstrate the availability of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites as drug carrier for cancer cell killing.
## In vitro and in vivo MR imaging
The high transverse relaxivity of the synthesized $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O-}$ 66 core-shell composites makes it potential as a contrast agent for cancer diagnosis. The $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighted MR images of HeLa cells incubated with different concentrations of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites (0, 25, 50, 100, 150 and $200~\mathrm{{mg/L})}$ ) became much darker with increasing the concentration of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ due to the dose-dependent cellular uptake. The results demonstrate the capability of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites as $\mathsf{T}_{2}\mathsf{-}$ - weighted MR contrast agent for in vitro MR imaging (Fig. 5A).
The feasibility of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites for in vivo MR imaging was also tested. A significant darkening effect was observed in the liver region of the Kunming mouse at $10\mathrm{\min}$ post-injection of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites $400\upmu\upiota$ , $5~{\mathrm{mg/mL}}$ , $24\:\:\mathrm{\mg}\:\:\mathsf{F e/k g})$ , indicating the ability of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ to enhance in vivo $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighted images (Fig. 5B). The dramatic $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ signal intensity decrease is probably due to the phagocytosis of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites by the liver macrophage cells in reticuloendothelial systems (RES)55. Considering the excellent MR imaging capability of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites, MR imaging of HeLa tumor-bearing mice was then carried out. After being intravenously injected with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 composites, remarkable darkening effect was observed in the tumor area just $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ post-injection and the MR image became even darker at $^{\mathfrak{g}\mathfrak{h}}$ post-injection (Fig. 5C). The quantified $\bar{\mathsf{T}}_{2^{-}}$ weighted MR signals in the tumor also showed a gradual decrease over 9 h post-injection, demonstrating the tumor accumulation of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites, which is probably due to the enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect of tumors.
## Biodistribution and toxicology studies
The potential toxic effect of the nanomaterials is a major concern for biomedical application. Thus, the metabolism, biodistribution and long-term toxicity of the as-synthesized $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ core-shell composites were systematically investigated. For metabolism study, the $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighted images of the Kunming mouse injected with the composites were collected after 1 day, 7days, 14 days and 30 days of injection. Strong darkening effect after injection and signal recovery at 30 days post-injection were observed in the liver region but no time-dependent darkening effect was observed in the urinary bladder, indicating that $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ was not excreted from renal but from liver (Fig. 5B). For biodistribution study, the major organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney) of the mice treated with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 for 1 day, 7 days and 30 days were collected, weighted and solubilized by aqua regia for AAS measurement of Fe and ICP-MS determination of $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ concentrations, respectively. As expected and consistent with the $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighted MR images, high levels of Fe and Zr contents mainly accumulated in mononuclear phagocyte system such as spleen and liver (Fig. 6A and Fig. S10, ESI†). The Fe levels in all measured organs sustainably decreased as the post-injection time prolonged and nearly dropped back to the normal levels after 30 days expect liver, in which the Fe content was slightly higher than the control. It was noteworthy that Zr was not detected in heart, lung and kidney due to the absence of Zr in organism and no accumulation of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ in these organs. In addition, the Zr levels in liver and spleen could be gradually metabolized over time, which was consistent with the biodistribution of Fe.
For in vivo long-term toxicity, the body weight and the blood analysis were evaluated. The body weights of the control group and the experimental group treated with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ maintained similar increase over 30 days, and no death and significant body weight drop were observed in the experimental group, illustrating the injection of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites had no perceivable interference on the growth of mice (Fig. S11, ESI†). Blood analysis parameters including liver function markers (total protein, TP; albumin, ALB; globulin GLB; alanine aminotransferase, ALT; aspartate aminotransferase, AST; alkaline phosphatase, ALKP; gamma glutamyl transaminase, GGT; total bilirubin, TBIL; direct bilirubin, DBIL) and kidney function markers (urea, UREA; creatinine, CREA; uric acid URIC) at different time points postinjection of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ appeared to be normal compared with those in the control group, indicating no obvious liver and kidney disorders were induced by the injection of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ (Fig. 6B). All these results demonstrated that the as-synthesized $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 was a relatively safe theranostic agent for biomedical application.
## In vivo antitumor efficacy
Inspired by the biocompatibility of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 and in vitro anticancer effect of the DOX-loaded $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ , the in vivo chemotherapy performance of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX was investigated using the HeLa tumor-bearing nude mice. As shown in Fig. 7A, the tumors in the mice treated with PBS grew quickly. In contrast, the tumor growth on mice treated with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX was effectively inhibited due to the preferential DOX accumulation and release in tumor site via EPR effect. Furthermore, the photos and the sizes of the tumor blocks isolated after treated 30 days showed that the mice treated with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX had the smallest tumor size (Fig. 7B and 7C). All these results make a proof of concept that $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ could efficiently carry and release drugs into tumor, leading to effective antitumor efficacy.
Although visualized tumor size could be observed from the photos of the mice and tumor blocks, adopting non-invasive MR imaging to monitor tumor development is necessary to realize precision medicine. Thus, monitoring the tumor change of the mice after treated with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX via MR imaging was carried out to accurately evaluate the antitumor efficacy. The MR images of HeLa-tumor bearing mice injected with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX $200~\upmu\upiota,$ $5\ \mathrm{mg/mL}$ ) showed obvious darkening effect in the tumor region, indicating that the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX could passively accumulated into the tumor at 7 days post-injection (Fig. 7D and Fig. S12, $\mathsf{E S l^{+}}\mathrm{:}$ . Continuous monitoring of tumor development by MR imaging was performed, and efficient tumor growth inhibition in the mice treated with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX was observed through the obtained MR images at 21 days post-injection. In contrast, the tumors of the mice with the treatment of PBS grew rapidly as revealed by MR images. All the results not only further convincingly demonstrated the efficient antitumor efficacy of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX in term of MR imaging, but also indicated the great potential of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ in imagingguided therapy.
## Conclusions
In conclusion, we developed a novel $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66 theranostic agent by in situ growth of UiO-66 MOF shell on $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ core. The obtained $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ core-shell composites can serve as nanocarrier and contrast agent for simultaneous drug delivery and $\mathsf{T}_{2}$ -weighted MR imaging. The exceptional high drug loading capacity $(\sim63~\mathsf{w t\%}$ , $2.0\ \mathsf{m g}$ DOX per mg composites), sustainable and effective drug release make $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ an excellent drug delivery carrier. Moreover, high transverse relaxivity $(255.87\ \mathsf{m M}^{-1}\ \mathsf{s}^{-1})$ revealed that $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ has the ability as contrast agent for MR imaging. The cytotoxicity assay, biodistribution and in vivo toxicology studies demonstrated that the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ composites possess low toxicity and good biocompatibility, which inspire us to explore their antitumor efficiency and MR imaging capability in vitro and in vivo. High cancer cell mortality, remarkable tumor size inhibition and significant darkening effect were obtained after treatment with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{U i O}\cdot66$ or $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@$ UiO-66-DOX in vitro and in vivo. All the results indicate that the presented novel multifunctional MOF-based composites should be very promising in cancer therapy and diagnosis due to their effective drug delivery and MR imaging.
